[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:03] Understood. I don't have competitor pricing. Beyond pricing, supply contracts and waste, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me as many distinct ideas as you can.

[candidate · 1:11] No competitor pricing — I'll assume they've got at least mid-single-digit room given specialty positioning, and flag it as a thing to test.

Other levers, beyond price, procurement and waste:

Menu mix — push customers toward high-margin items. Espresso drinks and brewed coffee carry better margins than food and dairy-heavy drinks. Reformat the menu board, bundle, and make the house drink the default.

Portion and recipe engineering — smaller default milk pour, cheaper alt-milk base, standardize shot weights across stores.

Substitution — reformulate the bean blend, use a lower-cost origin in blended drinks while keeping the premium single-origin as a visible halo.

Surcharges — charge for oat and almond milk, syrups, extra shots. That's effectively a price increase without touching headline prices.

Vertical integration or direct trade on beans to cut the middleman.

Hedging — forward contracts on coffee and dairy to stop riding spot.

Overhead — it's up two points; trim corporate and marketing.

Store portfolio — close or renegotiate rent on the worst-performing locations.

Loyalty program to lift ticket size rather than just traffic.
