[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 1:12] A broad list of levers. Pulling it together, what is your recommendation to the CEO, and which actions would you sequence first?

[candidate · 1:20] My recommendation: Brew & Bean's margin decline is almost entirely an input-cost problem that was never passed through to customers, and the fix is to raise prices now while fixing procurement underneath.

The support: of the 18-point margin decline, 16 points are COGS and 2 are overhead — labor is flat. Within COGS, non-bean inputs like dairy, packaging and food drove about 11.8 points and beans about 4.2. Menu prices haven't moved in two years, so none of that was absorbed. Meanwhile revenue grew 19% on volume, which means growth is compounding the problem.

Sequencing: first, a price increase in the next quarter — even 8-10% recovers most of the gap and requires no operational change. Second, lock in bean and dairy contracts and add alt-milk surcharges. Third, menu mix and portion standardization. Fourth, trim the two points of overhead.

Risks: volume elasticity is unknown without competitor pricing, so I'd pilot the increase in a subset of stores before rolling to all 200.
